course: dictionary
lesson: 5414
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning. The word lace can mean a string for tying shoes, or a fine cloth with holes in it. To know which meaning is right, read the words around it. Those words are its context, and they leave clues.
explain: «Knots in laces hold only because the lace rubs on itself.» Knots and rubbing tell you that lace is the thin string on a shoe. A lace cloth would not be tied in knots like that.
explain: «On an escalator, stand still, hold the handrail, keep feet in the middle, tie your laces, ...» Here tie your laces is the clue. You tie a string, so lace means the string.
explain: Lace can also be a delicate cloth with patterns of holes, like the lace curtains in a window. Lace up is different again: it means to tie your shoes or boots. Always check your meaning by putting it back into the sentence.
fact: Lace is a noun and a verb, so it can be a thing or an action.
fact: A lace, or shoelace, is a thin string used to tie a shoe or boot.
fact: Lace is a fine, delicate cloth with patterns of holes, used on dresses, curtains and tablecloths.
fact: To lace up means to tie shoes or boots with laces.
fact: To lace food or drink means to add a small amount of something to it, often secretly.
exercise: «Knots in laces hold only because the lace rubs on itself.» What does lace mean here? | *A thin string used to tie a shoe | A tall, thin tree | A fine cloth with patterns of holes | To add a small amount of something to food
exercise: «He had to lace up his boots before the walk.» What does lace up mean? | To lace food or drink | To wash the boots | To take the boots off | *To tie shoes or boots with laces
exercise: «On an escalator, ... tie your laces, keep scarves and long clothes away from the edges...» What might happen to untied laces on an escalator? | *They could get caught | They could help you hold the handrail | They would make the escalator go faster | They might turn into a cloth
exercise: Ellie says lace in «lace curtains» means a shoelace. Is she right? | Yes, curtains are tied with laces | *No, here lace means a fine cloth with patterns of holes | Yes, to lace up means to tie curtains | No, lace means to dance
test: «They are not for cleaning shoes; they gently remind your feet to keep away from the gap, where laces, soft wellies and long scarves can be caught.» What can be caught in the gap? | The shoe polish | A cup of tea | *Laces | The handrail
test: «Three breaks in a row she stands near the rope, pretending to tie her shoelace, until the lace is nearly worn through.» What does shoelace mean? | *A thin string for tying a shoe | To add something to a drink | A fine cloth with holes | A rope for climbing
exam: Ellie says lace in «lace curtains» means to tie. What is the best answer? | She is wrong, lace means to sneeze | She is right, it means a rope | *She is wrong, lace here is a fine cloth with patterns of holes | She is right, it means to tie
